  "strategy": "We are to minimize the objective function: 2 * n * max(conv(a, a)) / (sum(a))^2 constrained by sum(a) >= 0.01 and 0 <= a_i <= 1000.\n\nI propose a novel approach using adaptive-variable step function generation: we will dynamically adjust the sequence length and structure using iterative optimization with auto-convolution bound tracking. The key innovation is a hybrid gradient-linear method that organizes the search in two main phases across different sequence lengths to ensure global minimization.\n\nApproach and rationale:\n1. We use an iterative method that alternates between gradient-based descent and linear programming (LP) optimizations with varying sequence lengths.\n2. This method starts from the parent sequence (if available) and if not, uses a default uniform sequence of length 800.\n3. During each optimization round, we systematically vary the sequence length from floor(n/2) to 2*n, applying both energy-minimization gradient descent and peak-focused linear programming to ensure downward pressure on the objective.\n4. We then use a bootstrap mutation process to explore different configuration spaces: for each kept element, we introduce a random change with exponential mutation to simulate evolution-like exploration.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Sequence representation: a[0..n-1] (length \"n\")\n- Objective: minimize F(a,n) = 2*n * max_{k} conv(a,a)[k] / (sum(a))^2\n- Constraints: sum(a) >= 0.01, 0 <= a_i <= 1000\n- Auto-convolution: standard full linear convolution (2n-1 terms)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - If parent sequence is available and valid, use it cast to float\n   - Else, default to uniform sequence of length 800\n   - Evaluate original candidate and store as best_seque\n\n2. Preprocessing:\n   - Normalize current sequence to sum=1.0\n   - Protect against negatives and values >1000\n\n3. Cooldown Period:\n   - If no initial parent, run through all steps but at reduced intensity\n\n4. Core Optimization Loop:\n   a) Gradient Phase (energy minimization):\n      - Apply FFT-based gradient descent to the current sequence for energy mode\n      - Target: minimize the energy (magnitude squared) of the auto-convolution\n\n   b) Linear Programming Phase:\n      - Formulate the sequence as a normalized probability distribution\n      - Compute constraints: the convolution must not exceed its max bound\n      - Solve convex relaxation:\n        min M\n        subject to: conv(a,a)[k] <= M\n                    sum_i a_i = 1\n                    a_i >= 0, M >= 0\n\n   c) Peak Targeting Phase:\n      - Generate a new sequence candidate with dual initialization (current and LP-optimal)\n      - Use exponential transfer function to blend both solutions\n      - Recompute max bound and attempt to push M lower\n\n   d) Sequence Length Variation:\n      - Double the sequence length and inject zeros while maintaining normalization\n      - Explore higher-dimensional space with same sum constraint\n      - Monitor the objective's sensitivity to gap size in convolution\n\n   e) Mutation-Based Bootstrapping:\n      - Generate modified child candidate by applying random mutation to best_seq\n      - Mutation depth: exponential distribution with mean=0.5 * max(a)\n      - Compare against best known, update if improvement found\n\n5. Output Selection:\n   - Track improvement during all phases\n   - Maintain multiple candidates from different conservation regions\n   - Return the sequence with minimal objective as a normalized list\n\nSearchChoices and Hyperparameters:\n- If valuable running time, we can arbitrarily increase objective evaluation frequency\n- Keep the sequence length fixed near the parent's dimension (typically 800) unless improvement stalls\n- Exponential mutation factor: gamma=0.7 (controls structure change freedom)",
